Below is a **self‑contained console program** that shows the two most common ways you’ll encounter the new `Span<T>` / `Memory<T>` types in C# 8+.  
The program is annotated so you can see exactly **what each line is doing** and why you would choose one type over the other.

```csharp
// <SnippetSpanMemoryDemo.cs>
using System;
using System.Buffers;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("=== Span<T> vs Memory<T> demo ===\n");

        /* 1.  Span<T> – stack‑only, zero‑allocation, perfect for short‑lived work */
        int[] numbers = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
        // Get a Span that references the underlying array – no copy.
        Span<int> numSpan = numbers.AsSpan();

        // Slice the span to look only at the first 3 elements.
        Span<int> firstThree = numSpan.Slice(0, 3);
        // Mutate the underlying array through the span.
        firstThree[1] = 42;            // changes numbers[1]
        Console.WriteLine("Array after span mutation: " + string.Join(", ", numbers));

        /* 2.  Span<T> – stack‑allocated buffer (no heap allocation at all) */
        // Allocate 10 chars on the stack.
        Span<char> buffer = stackalloc char[10];
        // Copy a string literal into the stack buffer
        "Hello".CopyTo(buffer);
        Console.WriteLine("Stack buffer contains: " + buffer.ToString());

        /* 3.  Memory<T> – heap‑based, can be stored, passed across async/await boundaries */
        // Allocate a byte buffer that lives on the heap
        Memory<byte> heapMemory = new byte[64];
        // Get a Span from the Memory to work with it immediately
        Span<byte> heapSpan = heapMemory.Span;
        // Fill the buffer with a pattern
        for (int i = 0; i < heapSpan.Length; i++) heapSpan[i] = (byte)i;

        // Pass the Memory to an async method that reads it
        int sum = await SumAsync(heapMemory);
        Console.WriteLine($"Sum of bytes in Memory: {sum}");

        /* 4.  Memory<T> – can be stored in a field, struct, or class */
        var holder = new BufferHolder(heapMemory);
        // Call a method that uses Span internally, but the Memory lives on the heap.
        holder.ProcessFirstHalf();

        /* 5.  Span<T> – cannot be stored on the heap */
        // The following would *not* compile:
        // Span<int> badSpan = new int[10].AsSpan();   // ❌
        // Span<int> anotherBad = new Span<int>(heapMemory); // ❌
        // Reason: a Span is a ref struct, so it can only live on the stack.

        /* 6.  Using MemoryMarshal to obtain a reference to the first element */
        ref byte firstByte = ref MemoryMarshal.GetReference(heapMemory.Span);
        Console.WriteLine($"First byte by ref: {firstByte}");
    }

    /* Example async method that consumes Memory<byte> */
    static async Task<int> SumAsync(Memory<byte> data)
    {
        // Pretend we need a delay (e.g., network I/O).
        await Task.Delay(10);

        int sum = 0;
        foreach (var b in data.Span) sum += b;
        return sum;
    }
}

/* Helper class that owns a Memory<T> and exposes a method that works with Span<T> */
public class BufferHolder
{
    private readonly Memory<byte> _buffer;

    public BufferHolder(Memory<byte> buffer) => _buffer = buffer;

    // The method can freely use Span<T> while the Memory<T> stays on the heap.
    public void ProcessFirstHalf()
    {
        // Work with a slice of the buffer as a Span<T>
        Span<byte> firstHalf = _buffer.Span.Slice(0, _buffer.Span.Length / 2);

        // Example operation: reverse the first half in place
        Array.Reverse(firstHalf.ToArray()); // (just for demo; normally use a custom reverse)

        Console.WriteLine("First half after reverse: " + BitConverter.ToString(firstHalf.ToArray()));
    }
}
// </SnippetSpanMemoryDemo.cs>
```

### What the code demonstrates

| Feature | What the code shows | Why you’d use it |
|---------|---------------------|------------------|
| `Span<T>` | `numbers.AsSpan()` → slice → modify underlying array | Zero‑allocation, short‑lived work; ideal for parsing or in‑place manipulation |
| `stackalloc` | `stackalloc char[10]` → no heap allocation | Very small buffers that live only during the method call |
| `Memory<T>` | `new byte[64]` → passed to async method | Stores a reference‑type buffer that can survive async/await or be stored in a field |
| `Memory<T>` + `Span<T>` | `heapMemory.Span` inside async method | Get a span for fast operations while the memory lives on the heap |
| `Memory<T>` field | `BufferHolder` | Demonstrates that a `Memory<T>` can be a member of a class/struct |
| `MemoryMarshal.GetReference` | Get a `ref byte` to the first element | Allows unsafe‑style direct reference access without copying |
| Compiler restrictions | The commented lines that don’t compile | Emphasises that `Span<T>` cannot be stored on the heap (it’s a `ref struct`) |

**Key take‑away**  
- Use **`Span<T>`** for *fast, stack‑only* operations.  
- Use **`Memory<T>`** when you need to *persist the buffer*, store it in a field, or pass it through async boundaries.  
- Under the hood, both types simply expose a `ReadOnlySpan<T>`/`Span<T>` view of an existing array, `stackalloc` buffer, or any other contiguous memory source.  

Feel free to paste this file into a new C# 8/9/10/11 console project and run it to see the output!
